An inferred polymorphic callback keeps its enclosing result type variable
sig fromPair : forall a. (forall b. b -> (b, a)) -> a fun fromPair(f) { f(42).2 } fromPair(fun(x) { (x, id) })
args : --set=recheck_frontend_transformations=true --set=typecheck_ir=true --set=fail_on_ir_type_error=true
stdout : fun : [] ((a::Any) -> a::Any)

One callback result still has a monotype
sig fromPair : forall a. (forall b. b -> (b, a)) -> a fun fromPair(f) { f(42).2 } var f = fromPair(fun(x) { (x, id) }); (f(42), f(true))
args : --set=recheck_frontend_transformations=true --set=typecheck_ir=true --set=fail_on_ir_type_error=true
stderr : @.*Type error: Expected Int, got Bool.*
exit : 1

A source callback abstraction still needs an annotation for an unresolved type
sig fromPair : forall a. (forall b. b -> (b, a)) -> a fun fromPair(f) { f(42).2 } fromPair(/\ [b]. { fun(x) { (x, id) } })
args : --set=recheck_frontend_transformations=true --set=typecheck_ir=true --set=fail_on_ir_type_error=true
stderr : @.*Type error: Type abstraction needs a type annotation.*
exit : 1
